A||||7||mshill||Testing subwoofer system...||||5||
Z||000000||mshill||01-07-2000||04:07 PM||||I am pulling a subwoofer system out of my 86GT (kit car donor) for sale.  My dillema is that I don't deel comfortable offering it for sale if I cannot test it (the car doesn't have a radio in it).<P>Any ideas on how to test a sub-woofer system outside the car?||||reg||5
Z||000001||mrfiero||01-07-2000||10:40 PM||||You can test a subwoofer system even if it has already been removed from the car.  Plug everything together (amp, speaker, slider, radio, etc.) and plug the end into the fuse block.  It's just like having it installed, but not in it's "proper" place.<P>This should at least tell you if the amp and/or speaker is still good.   <P>What were you thinking about asking for it?||||reg||1
Z||000002||862M4inCA||01-08-2000||06:56 AM||||I am interested in locating a stock sub, however I just want to know if it will be compatible with my existing stock radio. This was the "base" model with just AM/FM and no cassette player. Does the sub somehow patch into the wiring harness from the radio, or does is need to make a couple connections directly to a plug located on the radio? Also would I just be better off working with an aftermarket solution. Any info is appreciated.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||01-08-2000||07:17 AM||||862m4inCA--If you're gonna get a sub, why not invest in an upgraded stereo?  You can find one of the stock, EQ, knobless ones fairly cheap if you look around.  I think SuperX or Fierodave has/had one for sale, too.  Or you can buy a new/used one from AATechnologies ( <A HREF="http://www.aatechnologies.com" TARGET=_blank>www.aatechnologies.com</A>   ) for $130.  It's not new, but reconditioned and a vast improvement over what you have.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||01-08-2000||05:11 PM||||The factory subwoofer system is compatible with all stock Fiero stereos, from the base am/fm radio to the upgraded am/fm cass. w/EQ.  The subwoofer harness plugs into the radio and no real modifications are need....just plug and play.<P>You could certainly make a better system from aftermarket components, but the factory set-up is very simple to install and works just fine.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||william_thorniii||01-09-2000||03:03 AM||||mshill,<P>The next time that you go to throw out your computer tear it apart and salvage the power supply.  They run on 17V, luckily it is broken up into 12V and 5V.  That way you can set it all up on your dining room table, not the garage floor. <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
Z||000006||mshill||01-09-2000||04:57 AM||||Unfortunately, I don't have a stock radio (one of the reasons I will be selling the sub-system).  So, it sounds like if I have a 12V supply and a pre-amp output from some source (radio, CD) I just hook it all up and go.  Would the headphone output of a portable CD or radio work?<P>mrfiero and 862M4inCA, I am going to ask $200 when I get the thing out and tested.||||reg||1
Z||000007||william_thorniii||01-09-2000||07:23 PM||||mshill,<P>I guess a portable CD player would work with the proper wiring.  I am not familiar with the factory set up to determine what type of wiring you would need.  Chances are if you hook up the amp and sub only you should be able to hear feedback (thump) when you first apply power.  <P>Good Luck,<P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||3
